\section{\texorpdfstring{\pkg{cSEM}}{cSEM} R Illustration}
\label{sec:illustration}
\subsection{Getting started with \pkg{cSEM}}

To use the \pkg{cSEM} package, users first need to install \textsf{R} and an integrated development environment (IDE) such as \textsf{RStudio}.
For the installation, we refer to the official guides for \textsf{R} (\url{https://cran.r-project.org}) and \textsf{RStudio} (\url{https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop}).

When \textsf{RStudio} is opened for the first time, it displays four panes (Figure~\ref{fig:rstudio}).
The two most important ones are the \emph{script editor} (upper left), where code is written and saved, and the \emph{Console} (lower left), where the code is executed and results are printed.
The \emph{Environment} pane (upper right) lists the objects currently loaded into memory, and the fourth pane (lower right) provides access to files, plots, and help pages.

Although code can be typed directly into the console, it is good practice to work in a script, since a script can be saved, shared, and re-run, which is essential for reproducible analyses.
To create a new script, click \textsf{File~$\rightarrow$ New File~$\rightarrow$ R Script}.
The typical workflow is to write code in the editor and run the selected line(s) with \texttt{Ctrl~+~Enter}.
Any line beginning with \texttt{\#} is treated as a comment: it documents what the code does and is ignored when the code runs.

<<label = intro-comment>>=
# This is a comment and is not executed.
1 + 1   # Code is executed; the result is printed in the Console.
@

Before \pkg{cSEM} can be used, it must be installed. The \pkg{cSEM} package is available on CRAN, so the most recent stable version can be installed with:

<<install-csem, eval = FALSE >>=
install.packages("cSEM")
@

Installation only needs to be done once per machine (rerun this line to update the package later).
Moreover, development versions can be installed from GitHub: \url{https://github.com/FloSchuberth/cSEM}.
After installation, the package must be loaded at the start of every new \textsf{R} session before its functions become available:

<<load-csem, message=FALSE>>=
library(cSEM)
@

With \pkg{cSEM} installed and loaded, we can now import the data and specify the model, as described in the following sections.

\input{LaTeX/plots/rstudio_test.tex}

\subsection{Model and Data}
\label{sec:model-data}
The \pkg{cSEM} package \citep{csem} offers a variety of composite-based approaches to SEM including PLS-PM and PLSc.
Following \citet{Cheah2026}, our illustration of the application of PLSc using \pkg{cSEM} draws on the employee retention model introduced by \citet[Chapter~13]{Hair2019c}. 

The model explains the effects of organizational commitment~(OC) and job satisfaction~(JS) on employees' staying intentions~(SI), with work environment perceptions~(EP) and attitudes toward coworkers~(AC) as exogenous antecedent constructs.
Five constructs are reflectively measured by a total of 21 indicators.
A detailed description of the indicators can be found in \citet{Cheah2026}.
The model is presented in Figure \ref{fig:retentionmodel}.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{"LaTeX/plots/retention_model_preview.png"}
    \caption{Employee Retention Model}
    \label{fig:retentionmodel}
\end{figure}

The dataset used for model estimation is publicly available from the SmartPLS website: \url{https://www.smartpls.com/documentation/sample-projects/employee-retention}. 
The synthetic dataset comprises $N = 400$ observations from HBAT Industries and was generated to satisfy the assumptions of factor-based SEM, including multivariate normality.
For this tutorial, we assume the data has been downloaded as a CSV file.
After downloading the dataset, it needs to be loaded into the R Environment.
The Employee Retention dataset is stored as an CSV File using ';' as the separator, thus it can be loaded into R using following command:

<<load-data>>=
# Import dataset
HBAT_SEM <- read.csv("data/HBAT_SEM.csv", sep=";")

# Select relevant variables
HBAT_SEM_rel <- HBAT_SEM[, c("AC1", "AC2", "AC3", "AC4",
                   "EP1", "EP2", "EP3", "EP4",
                   "JS1", "JS2", "JS3", "JS4", "JS5",
                   "OC1", "OC2", "OC3", "OC4",
                   "SI1", "SI2", "SI3", "SI4")]

# show dimensions of the relevant dataset
dim(HBAT_SEM_rel)

# count missing values per variable
colSums(is.na(HBAT_SEM_rel))
@

Since \pkg{cSEM} can only handle complete datasets, i.e., datasets without any missing values, we check the number of missing values per variable using the \fn{is.na()} function.
The output shows that the variables AC4 and EP4 each contain one missing values.
Before we can continue, we thus need to either apply imputation methods or delete the incomplete observations.
Deleting incomplete observations can be done via following command:

<<missing-data>>=
# filter the dataset, only select complete cases
HBAT_SEM_rel <- HBAT_SEM_rel[complete.cases(HBAT_SEM_rel),]

#  the number of rows changed from 400 to 398 
dim(HBAT_SEM_rel)
@

Since two observations contain missing values, all analyses in the remainder of this tutorial are based on the $N = 398$ complete observations.
When using their own data, users must ensure it is stored as a data frame or a matrix before passing it to \pkg{cSEM} functions; if necessary, the data can be converted with \fn{as.data.frame()} or \fn{as.matrix()}.

\subsection{Model specification}
In \pkg{cSEM}, models are specified using the \pkg{lavaan} model syntax.
The \pkg{lavaan} package does not need to be installed to use the syntax. 
In particular, the \code{=\textasciitilde} and the \code{<\textasciitilde} operators are used to define the relations between the constructs and their indicators.
Specifically, the \code{=\textasciitilde} operator is used to specify reflective measurement models, while the \code{<\textasciitilde} is used to specify composite models \citep[see][for an explanation of reflective and composite models]{}.
In addition, the \code{\textasciitilde} operator is used to specify the relations among constructs.
In case, an observed variable should be directly accounted for in the structural model, it must be specified as a single indicator construct, as common in PLS-PM \citep{}.
The \pkg{cSEM} specification of the retention model illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:retentionmodel} is as follows:

<<model-spec>>=
model <- "
  # Measurement model
  AC =~ AC1 + AC2 + AC3 + AC4
  EP =~ EP1 + EP2 + EP3 + EP4
  JS =~ JS1 + JS2 + JS3 + JS4 + JS5
  OC =~ OC1 + OC2 + OC3 + OC4
  SI =~ SI1 + SI2 + SI3 + SI4

  # Structural model
  JS ~ EP + AC
  OC ~ EP + AC + JS
  SI ~ JS + OC
"
@

\subsection{Model estimation}
\label{sec:estimation}

The central function is \fn{csem()}.
The minimal input required for the \fn{csem()} function is a dataset and a model:

\begin{description}
  \item[\prm{.data}] A data frame or matrix with column names matching the indicator names used in the model description. 
  Possible column types of data proved are: "\code{logical}", "\code{numeric}" ("\code{double} or "\code{integer}"), "\code{factor}" ("\code{ordered} and/or "\code{unordered}"), "\code{character}" or a mix of several types.
  \item[\prm{.model}] A model string written in \pkg{lavaan} syntax, with \code{=\~} for measurement relations and \texttt{\~} for structural (regression) relations.
\end{description}

By default weights are estimated using the partial leasts sqaures path modeling algorithm ("PLS-PM"). 

To ultimately estimate with the csem function, we call it and save the results as \code{res\_csem}:

<<csem-est>>=
# PLSc-SEM estimation
res_csem <- csem(
  .data  = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model = model
)
@

To obtain a comprehensive overview of all results, including loadings, path coefficients, reliability measures, and $R^2$ values, use:

<<summary-rescsem,  eval = TRUE, results='hide'>>=
# Full results summary
summary_res <- summarize(res_csem)
print(summary_res)
@

<<summary-rescsem-compact>>=
# For a more compact overview
summary_res$Estimates$Loading_estimates
summary_res$Estimates$Path_estimates
@

Note that the standard errors, $t$-values, and $p$-values in the \fn{summarize()} output are reported as \code{NA}.
The reason is that a plain \fn{csem()} call computes point estimates only.
To conduct statistical inference, the standard errors of the estimates must be obtained by a resampling procedure such as the bootstrap, which is introduced in Section~\ref{sec:assessment}.

\section{Assessment Methods}
\label{sec:assessment}

The next step involves assessing the model estimates, according to the guidelines described in \citet{Benitez2020}.
The first step involves testing for overall model fit. 
The second step addresses the assessment of the reflective measurement models. 
The third step evaluates the Structural model. 

\subsection{Overall model fit}
\label{sec:overall-fit}

In this section we test for overall model fit.
But before we need to ensure the estimation is valid. 
This is done with the \fn{verify()} function. 
This function gives the user details about the estimation. 
It gives information if convergence is achieved, all absolute standardized loading 
estimates are smaller or equal than one.
It also checks of the construct variance covariance matrix is positive semi-definite, if all reliability estimates are smaller or equal than one and if the model-implied indicator variance covarariance matrix is positive semi-definite. 
It also checks if the construct and the model-implied indicator variance covariance matrix are semi positive definite and if all reliability estimates are smaller or equal than one.
If a check fails, the results should not be interpreted and the user needs to customize the algorithm to ensure a valid estimation process.
For that check Section \ref{sec:Customization}.
<<verify>>=
# Check whether the estimation is admissible
verify(res_csem)
@


After that we can examine the overall model fit with the function \fn{testOMF()}. 
%For that we examine the $SRMR$, $d_{ULS}$, squared Eucliedean distance, and $d_G$, geodestic distance.
The function \fn{testOMF()} is a boostrap-based test for model fit originally proposed by \citet{Beran}. 
See also \citet{DijkstraandHenseler2015} who first suggested the test in the context of PLS-PM.
It outputs various overall model fit indices, such as the $d\_G$, $SRMR$:
<<test-omf>>=
# Bootstrap-based test of the overall model fit
fit_test <- testOMF(
  res_csem,
  .R                    = 1000,
  .seed                 = 42,
  .handle_inadmissibles = "replace"
)

fit_test
@
By default \fn{testOMF()} test the null hypothesis that the population indicator correlation matrix equals the population model-implied indicator correlation matrix. 
\fn{testOMF()} uses different distance measures to assess the distance between the sample indicator correlation matrix and the estimated model-implied indicator correlation matrix, namely the geodesic distance ($d_G$), the squared Euclidean distance $d_{ULS}$ and the root mean square residual $SRMR$.

\subsection{Assessment of the reflective measurement models}
\label{sec:measurement}



\subsubsection*{Evaluating the reliability of the construct scores}

The \fn{assess()} function computes all relevant quality criteria

<<assess-quality, excerpt.lines = 5>>=
#Compute all quality criteria
quality <- assess(res_csem, .inference = "asymptotic")
quality
@

% TODO (Jason): text — one or two sentences on the output above: printing `quality`
% shows all criteria grouped in titled sections; the output here is shortened for
% presentation ("..." marks omitted lines), running the code yields the full report.
% In the following, the criteria are extracted from `quality` one at a time.


<<assess-rho-A>>=
#Show Dijkstras and Henselers rho A
quality$Reliability
@

The results indicate that all constructs meet the commonly accepted thresholds.
All constructs succeed the recommend threshold of $0.707$ for Dijkstra-Henseler $\rho_a$, indicating reliable construct scores.
Additionnaly \fn{assess()} outputs alternative reliability estimates.
  
<<assess-cronbach-alpha>>=
# Show cronbach alpha
quality$Reliability
@  

<<assess-rho_C>>=
# Show rho_C
quality$RhoC
quality$RhoC_mm
quality$RhoC_weighted
quality$RhoC_weighted_mm
@  

% TODO (Jason): text — instead of printing the full assess() output, each criterion is
% extracted in turn. Reliability of the construct scores: Dijkstra-Henseler's rho_A
% (the reliability used by the PLSc correction); alternative estimates (Cronbach's
% alpha, rho_C) also reported.

\subsubsection*{Evaluating indicator reliability}

% TODO (Jason): text — indicator reliability via the factor loading estimates,
% threshold 0.708 (loading^2 >= 0.5), OC1 case + content-validity argument.
% Significance of the loadings requires the bootstrap -> this is where the resampling
% promised in the estimation section is introduced.

<<csem-boot>>=
# Re-estimate with bootstrap resampling for inference
res_csem_boot <- csem(
  .data             = HBAT_SEM_rel,
  .model            = model,
  .resample_method  = "bootstrap",
  .R                = 1000,
  .seed             = 42
)
@

<<loadings-boot>>=
# Loading estimates incl. bootstrap std. errors, t-values and p-values
summarize(res_csem_boot)$Estimates$Loading_estimates
@

\subsubsection*{Evaluating convergent validity}

% TODO (Jason): text — AVE >= 0.5, relation to indicator loadings.

<<ave>>=
# Average variance extracted (AVE)
quality$AVE
@

The average variance extracted (AVE) for all constructs are above the recommended threshold of $0.5$, supporting convergent validity.

\subsubsection*{Evaluating discriminant validity}
To assess discriminant validity users can utilize the HTMT \citep{Henseler2015} which is calculated with the default options within the \fn{assess()} function.
There are several ways on testing for discriminant validity with the HTMT.
One can compare the HTMT with a threshold, common values are 0.85 and 0.9 \citep{Henseler2015}.
Or we can do inference by calculating the confidence interval bounds and comparing the upper limit with one.
For that the \prm{.inference} needs to be set on \code{TRUE}.
If one is bigger than the upper limit then discriminant validity is established.


% TODO (Jason): text — HTMT: heterotrait vs. monotrait correlations, thresholds
% 0.85 (conservative) / 0.90 (liberal); proper decision via the upper bound of the
% bootstrap confidence interval (res_csem_boot is available at this point).

<<htmt>>=
# Heterotrait-monotrait ratio of correlations (HTMT)
quality$HTMT
@


<<htmt-inference>>=
quality_inf <- assess(.object = res_csem, 
                      .inference = TRUE, 
                      .absolute = FALSE)
quality_inf$HTMT
@


\subsubsection*{Assessing multicollinearity among the indicators and Evaluating the weights}

% TODO (Jason/Florian): decide how to present this step — collinearity among indicators
% concerns composite (Mode B) measurement; in the retention model all constructs are
% reflective (Mode A), so no Mode B VIF values are computed for this example.
% Options: state that the check is not applicable here and show the code pattern only,
% or switch one construct to a composite for illustration.
% NOTE: element name below (quality$VIF_modeB) still to be verified against the cSEM
% documentation; chunk is eval=FALSE until this step is settled.

<<vif-indicators, eval = FALSE>>=
# VIF values for Mode B (composite) weights
quality$VIF_modeB
@

%\subsection*{Evaluating the weights}
